@import net.keyboardbuilders.keyboardbuilders.web.Head
@import net.keyboardbuilders.keyboardbuilders.web.SiteUrl
@param head: Head
@param site: SiteUrl

<%--
    Editing: plain HTML paragraphs. NO BACKTICKS anywhere in this file -- one closes the
    raw string this page is written in, and jte then reports the error on the wrong line.
--%>
@template.layout(head = head, site = site, content = @`
    <article class="about">
        <figure class="about-portrait">
            <img src="/fowlie.webp" width="400" height="400"
                 alt="A cartoon of fowlie holding a soldering iron and a keyboard PCB">
            <%-- The badge's empty speech bubble, filled in. Positioned over the
                 bubble in the picture, so it moves with it at every width. --%>
            <figcaption class="about-bubble">Found your next build yet?</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h1>Why this exists</h1>

        <p>
            Keyboard Builders is a searchable catalog of open-source keyboard designs,
            so you can find one that fits you and actually build it.
        </p>

        <p>
            I built my first keyboard in 2022 and
            <a href="https://fowlie.github.io/2022/08/15/my-first-keyboard-build" rel="noopener" target="_blank">blogged about it here</a>.
            What fascinated me most on the way down the rabbit hole was how much
            creativity there is among keyboard designers. But it was also frustrating: I
            spent ages finding the right design for me, only to find its parts were out
            of stock. (Keeping track of stock is on my list, but the site can't do that
            yet.)
        </p>

        <h2>How it works</h2>

        <p>
            A good structure isn't enough on its own. It needs data too, and there are
            a ton of designs out there, with new ones every week, so collecting it had to
            be automated. A set of scripts reads the facts straight out of each design's
            hardware and firmware sources. From those I fill the database, draw
            the layout silhouettes and render the PCBs. It all runs on a Hetzner server
            in Finland.
        </p>

        <p>
            My hope is to grow this into the biggest catalog of open-source keyboards
            out there, so builders can find designs that fit their own needs. I also aim
            to build a "configure this design" page where the system will validate each
            part against the design's own hardware.
        </p>

        <p>
            None of this would exist without the designers who share their work for
            free. Every design here links back to its author and its repository, and
            I'm grateful to every one of them.
        </p>

        <h2>How accurate is it?</h2>

        <p>
            Every fact on a design page is read from the design's own files: its PCB,
            its firmware and its README. Nothing is guessed. When the scripts can't find
            something, the page says "not recorded" rather than filling in a likely
            answer. The scripts still get things wrong sometimes, for example by pairing
            a board with another board's firmware, and I fix those by hand as they're
            found.
        </p>

        <p>
            Found a mistake, or a design that's missing? Write to me at
            <a href="mailto:hello@keyboardbuilders.net?subject=keyboardbuilders">hello@keyboardbuilders.net</a>.
            Every correction makes the catalog better for the next builder.
        </p>

        <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>

        <p>
            No tracking cookies. Designers who sign in with GitHub get a session cookie,
            and if they unpublish a design we keep their GitHub username with it. Your favorites, and your choice on the
            <a href="/settings">Settings</a> page, are kept in your browser. The visitor
            counter (<a href="https://www.goatcounter.com" rel="noopener" target="_blank">GoatCounter</a>)
            stores nothing on your device.
        </p>

        <h2>Supporting the site</h2>

        <p>
            I work on this in the evenings, often late into the night, and the server
            and storage cost money.
            If you want to support my work and help pay the server bill, you can
            <a href="https://ko-fi.com/keyboardbuilders" rel="noopener" target="_blank">buy me a beer</a>,
            or simply share the site with your friends.
        </p>
    </article>
`)
